e eigenvectors.\n\n- For $\\lambda_1 =2,$ we get \n![](images/10/10bbcec99a48d331c21836dc104a1db2db241e50a534baf0db23c138168c5663.png)\nSo, we get an eigenvector \n![](images/c2/c2bff2faa79d780c5f89b28e72468c84a388d50e8c566b80e5d5677f90a5e3af.png)\n\n- For $\\lambda_2 =-3,$ we get \n![](images/70/704493d5ac4f48c88a9c521c4e43c41acfd80b6d6959237944fbc0d638e6012f.png)\nSo, we get an eigenvector \n![](images/a7/a77312d93a804e69dabb70aff5bc7bbf9798781c728dfc50fff1440da86b5b5c.png)\n\nNow, we write down the general solution to the given linear system of differential equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & ={c}_{1}{v}_{1}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{1}t}+{c}_{2}{v}_{2}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{2}t} \\\\  & ={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{2t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-3t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to find the values of $c_1$ and $c_2,$ we substitute $t=0$ in the general solution and use the initial values:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(0) & =[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{0}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]{e}^{0} & =[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1}+{c}_{2} \\\\ {c}_{1}+6{c}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1}+{c}_{2}=5 \\\\ {c}_{1}+6{c}_{2}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubtracting the first equation from the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5{c}_{2}=-5\\,⟹\\,{c}_{2}=-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $c_2 = -1$ into the first equation, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1}-1=5\\,⟹\\,{c}_{1}=6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we write down the solution to the initial value problem:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =6[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{2t}-[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-3t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534198 17592186045417 false] [17592186128953 1074 "Let's construct the matrix differential equation $\\mathbf{x}'(t)=A\\mathbf{x}(t)$ for the given system\n![](images/7a/7a07096a35f46528c18bdce5e711a5e813ac1923342e06cbff3f3c6b3215ae64.svg)\n\n- We are given that the eigenvalues are\n\n$$\n\\lambda_1= 2\n\\qquad\\text{and}\\qquad\n\\lambda_2= -3.\n$$\n\n- Let's go through the process of finding the eigenvectors.\n\n- For $\\lambda_1 =2,$ we get \n![](images/28/284acfc8cb4726bec4f4e805a8be0c037b38a0b9af287a27a4c7f5a2f1679d60.svg)\nSo, we get an eigenvector \n![](images/b3/b33588ca4b487e1de13aac19bdfc5347751dbe95a9f93c27729d7da54ab02e06.svg)\n\n- For $\\lambda_2 =-3,$ we get \n![](images/1c/1c6b0e7303099895b50f884468ba6bfb7830ec88bbaea17dbc322a5a70eeea4c.svg)\nSo, we get an eigenvector \n![](images/bb/bb4d152439d5a97dfda5196a24752e2048fb421a8cfac5c67ffbab512115d0c8.svg)\n\nNow, we write down the general solution to the given linear system of differential equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & ={c}_{1}{v}_{1}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{1}t}+{c}_{2}{v}_{2}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{2}t} \\\\  & ={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{2t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-3t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to find the values of $c_1$ and $c_2,$ we substitute $t=0$ in the general solution and use the initial values:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(0) & =[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{0}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]{e}^{0} & =[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1}+{c}_{2} \\\\ {c}_{1}+6{c}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1}+{c}_{2}=5 \\\\ {c}_{1}+6{c}_{2}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubtracting the first equation from the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5{c}_{2}=-5\\,⟹\\,{c}_{2}=-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $c_2 = -1$ into the first equation, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1}-1=5\\,⟹\\,{c}_{1}=6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we write down the solution to the initial value problem:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =6[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{2t}-[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-3t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534198 17592186045417 true] [17592186128954 1071 "Let $\\lambda_1=3$ and $\\lambda_2=-2$ be eigenvalues of the $2\\times 2$ matrix  $A$ that correspond to eigenvectors \n![](images/15/15d8764ce12b985a991ca9a0704ba037ccc29fa488a3f23ee643688ceebae60c.png) \nGiven that the position of a particle at time $t$ seconds is given by the vector $\\mathbf{x}(t)$ and that $\\mathbf{x}(t)$ is the solution to the initial value problem\n![](images/88/88cd1c87501899a5d6b49ce3fd1678fe9485300a2da638fb496f2c80b298ea86.png)\nfind the position of the particle after $2$ seconds." 13194139534198 17592186045417 false] [17592186128954 1071 "Let $\\lambda_1=3$ and $\\lambda_2=-2$ be eigenvalues of the $2\\times 2$ matrix  $A$ that correspond to eigenvectors \n![](images/be/bea45856123519703211eba01e1c6fe6b3cf8cd045eb7b2ad2a2a3a7cf9adc43.svg) \nGiven that the position of a particle at time $t$ seconds is given by the vector $\\mathbf{x}(t)$ and that $\\mathbf{x}(t)$ is the solution to the initial value problem\n![](images/62/62ced02d9670f7b749e19ea357508aeb57f6b65777d6c2dd5adc4d2e35172688.svg)\nfind the position of the particle after $2$ seconds." 13194139534198 17592186045417 true] [17592186129311 1038 #edb/ref 17592186413788 13194139534198 17592186045417 true] [17592186129311 1038 #edb/ref 17592186413789 13194139534198 17592186045417 true] [17592186129312 1038 #edb/ref 17592186413787 13194139534198 17592186045417 true] [17592186129312 1038 #edb/ref 17592186413791 13194139534198 17592186045417 true] [17592186129313 1038 #edb/ref 17592186413790 13194139534198 17592186045417 true] [17592186129399 1050 600.0 13194139534198 17592186045417 true] [17592186129578 1068 #edb/ref 17592186126953 13194139534198 17592186045417 false] [17592186129578 1068 #edb/ref 17592186126954 13194139534198 17592186045417 false] [17592186129578 1068 #edb/ref 17592186126955 13194139534198 17592186045417 false] [17592186129578 1068 #edb/ref 17592186126956 13194139534198 17592186045417 false] [17592186129578 1068 #edb/ref 17592186413772 13194139534198 17592186045417 true] [17592186129578 1068 #edb/ref 17592186413777 13194139534198 17592186045417 true] [17592186129578 1068 #edb/ref 17592186413781 13194139534198 17592186045417 true] [17592186129578 1068 #edb/ref 17592186413783 13194139534198 17592186045417 true] [17592186129578 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139534198 17592186045417 true] [17592186129578 1071 "Solve the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),\\,x(0)=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\ngiven that the general solution to our differential equation is\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nThe solution to the initial value problem is\n\n| $x(t)=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-1}} |  | $\\,\\,{e}^{t}+\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-2}} |  | $\\,\\,{e}^{-t}.$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | {{field-3}} |  |  | {{field-4}} |  |  |  |  |" 13194139534198 17592186045417 true] [17592186129578 1071 "Solve the initial value problem\n$x^{′}(t) = Ax(t), x(0) = \\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}$\ngiven that the general solution to our differential equation is\n$x(t) = c_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}e^{t} + c_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}e^{-t}, c_{1}, c_{2} ∈ R$.\n\nThe solution to the initial value problem is\n\n| $x(t) =$ |  | {{field-1}} |  | $e^{t} +$ |  | {{field-2}} |  | $e^{-t}$. |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n|  | {{field-3}} |  |  | {{field-4}} |  |  |  |  |" 13194139534198 17592186045417 false] [17592186129578 1074 "To find the values of ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}$, we substitute $t=0$ in the general solution:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(0) & =[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{0}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]{e}^{0} & =[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1}+2{c}_{2} \\\\ -{c}_{1}+2{c}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1}+2{c}_{2}=3 \\\\ -{c}_{1}+2{c}_{2}=-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding the equations, we get\n\n$$\n4{c}_{2}=2\\,⟹\\,{c}_{2}=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the first equation, we obtain \n\n$$\n{c}_{1}+2\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}=3\\,⟹\\,{c}_{1}=2.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =2\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{t}+\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-t} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]{e}^{t}+[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534198 17592186045417 true] [17592186129583 1068 #edb/ref 17592186126981 13194139534198 17592186045417 false] [17592186130360 1064 "An **initial value problem (IVP)** for a homogeneous linear system has the form\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}'(t)=A\\mathbf{x}(t),\n\\qquad \n\\mathbf{x}(t_0)=\\mathbf{x}_0,\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is a constant $2 \\times 2$ matrix and $\\mathbf{x}_0\\in\\mathbb{R}^2$ is the given initial state at $t=t_0.$ In other words, in an IVP we seek the solution of $\\mathbf{x}'(t)=A\\mathbf{x}(t)$ that passes through the point $\\mathbf{x}_0$ at $t=t_0.$\n\nTo solve an IVP, we proceed as follows:\n\n- Find the general solution of the system $\\mathbf{x}'(t)=A\\mathbf{x}(t){:}$\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}(t)=c_1\\mathbf{x}_1(t)+c_2\\mathbf{x}_2(t)\n$$\n\n- Substitute $t=t_0$ into the general solution and use the initial condition $\\mathbf{x}(t_0)=\\mathbf{x}_0{:}$\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}(t_0)=c_1\\mathbf{x}_1(t_0)+c_2\\mathbf{x}_2(t_0)\n\\qquad\\Rightarrow\\qquad\nc_1\\mathbf{x}_1(t_0)+c_2\\mathbf{x}_2(t_0)=\\mathbf{x}_0\n$$\n\n- Solve the resulting linear system for $c_1$ and $c_2,$ then substitute those values back into $\\mathbf{x}(t).$\n\nThe definition (and the procedure) above generalizes directly to the $n\\times n$ case.\n\nLet's see some concrete examples of solving an IVP." 13194139534198 17592186045417 false] [17592186130360 1064 "An **initial value problem (IVP)** for a homogeneous linear system has the form\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}'(t)=A\\mathbf{x}(t),\n\\qquad \n\\mathbf{x}(t_0)=\\mathbf{x}_0,\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is a constant $2 \\times 2$ matrix and $\\mathbf{x}_0\\in\\mathbb{R}^2$ is the given initial state at $t=t_0.$ In other words, in an IVP we seek the solution of $\\mathbf{x}'(t)=A\\mathbf{x}(t)$ that passes through the point $\\mathbf{x}_0$ at $t=t_0.$\n\nTo solve an IVP, we proceed as follows:\n\n- Find the general solution of the system $\\mathbf{x}'(t)=A\\mathbf{x}(t){:}$ \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}(t)=c_1\\mathbf{x}_1(t)+c_2\\mathbf{x}_2(t)\n$$\n\n- Substitute $t=t_0$ into the general solution and use the initial condition $\\mathbf{x}(t_0)=\\mathbf{x}_0{:}$\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}(t_0)=c_1\\mathbf{x}_1(t_0)+c_2\\mathbf{x}_2(t_0)\n\\qquad\\Rightarrow\\qquad\nc_1\\mathbf{x}_1(t_0)+c_2\\mathbf{x}_2(t_0)=\\mathbf{x}_0\n$$\n\n- Solve the resulting linear system for $c_1$ and $c_2,$ then substitute those values back into $\\mathbf{x}(t).$\n\nThe definition (and the procedure) above generalizes directly to the $n\\times n$ case.\n\nLet's see some concrete examples of solving an IVP." 13194139534198 17592186045417 true] [17592186413745 1080 "-2" 13194139534198 17592186045417 true] [17592186413745 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139534198 17592186045417 true] [17592186413745 1083 #uuid "0490d2f0-fe28-5967-9617-bbb17cd67f0b" 13194139534198 17592186045417 true] [17592186413746 1080 "2" 13194139534198 17592186045417 true] [1759218641374