 ={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}]{e}^{3t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{2t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to find the values of ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}$, we substitute $t=0$ in the general solution:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(0) & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 14\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}]{e}^{0}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{0} & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 14\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}3{c}_{2} \\\\ 7{c}_{1}-{c}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 14\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}3{c}_{2}=0 \\\\ 7{c}_{1}-{c}_{2}=14.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the first equation, we obtain \n\n$$\n{c}_{2}=0.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the second equation, we obtain \n\n$$\n7{c}_{1}-0=14\\,⟹\\,{c}_{1}=2.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the solution for the initial value problem is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =2\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}]{e}^{3t}+0\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{2t} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 14\\end{aligned}]{e}^{3t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534198 17592186045417 true] [17592186413788 1068 #edb/ref 17592186413773 13194139534198 17592186045417 true] [17592186413788 1068 #edb/ref 17592186413774 13194139534198 17592186045417 true] [17592186413788 1068 #edb/ref 17592186413778 13194139534198 17592186045417 true] [17592186413788 1068 #edb/ref 17592186413782 13194139534198 17592186045417 true] [17592186413788 1069 "q-326296" 13194139534198 17592186045417 true] [17592186413788 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139534198 17592186045417 true] [17592186413788 1071 "Solve the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),\\,x(0)=[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\ngiven that the general solution to our differential equation is\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{2t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nThe solution to the initial value problem is\n\n| $x(t)=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-1}} |  | $\\,\\,{e}^{2t}+\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-2}} |  | $\\,\\,{e}^{-t}.$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | {{field-3}} |  |  | {{field-4}} |  |  |  |  |" 13194139534198 17592186045417 true] [17592186413788 1072 #uuid "216d1aeb-34d0-53e9-bbe2-39e35d3f0d63" 13194139534198 17592186045417 true] [17592186413788 1074 "To find the values of ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}$, we substitute $t=0$ in the general solution:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(0) & =[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{0}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]{e}^{0} & =[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}3{c}_{1}+{c}_{2} \\\\ {c}_{1}+2{c}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}3{c}_{1}+{c}_{2}=5 \\\\ {c}_{1}+2{c}_{2}=5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying the first equation by $-2$ and adding the result to the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n-5{c}_{1}=-5\\,⟹\\,{c}_{1}=1.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the first equation, we obtain \n\n$$\n3\\cdot 1+{c}_{2}=5\\,⟹\\,{c}_{1}=2.\n$$\n\n \nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =1\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{2t}+2\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-t} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{2t}+[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534198 17592186045417 true] [17592186413789 1068 #edb/ref 17592186413776 13194139534198 17592186045417 true] [17592186413789 1068 #edb/ref 17592186413779 13194139534198 17592186045417 true] [17592186413789 1068 #edb/ref 17592186413780 13194139534198 17592186045417 true] [17592186413789 1068 #edb/ref 17592186413786 13194139534198 17592186045